{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "How can researchers measure partisan division in online speech at the level of a particular place, issue, and time, without mistaking ordinary differences in vocabulary for political disagreement?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We introduce a methodological framework to reliably measure polarization in public discourse at fine-grained resolution,\nleveraging the vast quantity of human-generated text available on digital platforms.", "page": 3},
          {"text": "Therefore, dissimilar language alone does not necessarily indicate polarized\npositions.", "page": 4}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Do partisan divisions over lockdowns, masks, and vaccines differ across U.S. states and Canadian provinces, and do those differences track regional voting patterns?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Our work addresses this need by providing a comprehensive, large-scale study of geographic and event-driven variation in online partisan polarization surrounding COVID-19 discussions across American states and Canadian provinces.", "page": 3},
          {"text": "we analyze millions of\ntweets to determine how polarization correlates with: (1) the ideological leanings of different regions;", "page": 3},
          {"text": "we compared the polarization observed in different American states and Canadian provinces over time for each topic", "page": 4}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Which political or public-health events coincide with peaks in polarization, and how quickly does the division rise and subside?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "we examined two types of events: 1) pre-selected political and\nvaccine-related events (Table 8, shown as vertical lines in Fig 13) and 2) highly polarized events detected through analysis of the highest peaks in polarization", "page": 16},
          {"text": "To evaluate the overall responsiveness of our measure to polarizing events, we computed\naverage polarization scores across topics (Fig 14A,B) and performed event-triggered averaging.", "page": 19}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Does regional polarization about vaccines before widespread rollout correspond to later vaccination uptake?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Next, we explore the relationship between vaccine-related polarization and actual vaccination rates\nacross US states.", "page": 13},
          {"text": "We used officially reported vaccination rates from one year after our Twitter\ndata collection period, as COVID-19 vaccines were only created and approved at the very end of our initial data\ncollection.", "page": 5}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Who discusses conspiracy theories, and does the volume of that discussion track daily partisan polarization?",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Finally, we explored the relationship between conspiracy discourse and polarization using time-resolved measurements\nof conspiracy-related tweets compared with aggregate daily polarization.", "page": 20},
          {"text": "we report the volume of conspiracy related tweets posted by users for the United States (A: affiliated with the Democrat and Republican party) and Canada", "page": 22}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Comparing how left- and right-leaning users talk about the same issue yields a scalable indicator of partisan division that can be tracked across places and dates.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "However, when examining discussions on the same contentious topic, language dissimilarity between different partisan\ngroups more likely reflects alternative semantic understandings of that specific topic—a phenomenon we hypothesize\ncorrelates strongly with polarization.", "page": 4},
          {"text": "While our study focuses on the COVID-19 context, the proposed\nmethodology for quantifying partisan polarization at scale can be adapted to examine large-scale discussions across\nvarious temporal, spatial, and topical dimensions.", "page": 3}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "There was no single pandemic-wide partisan divide. Its size depended on the intervention, the region’s politics, and the events under discussion; in the U.S. in particular, those differences also corresponded to later vaccination behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "we have demonstrated that\npolarization is not uniform but varies significantly by geography, political leaning, and public health topic, with substantial\ndifferences between the two countries.", "page": 25},
          {"text": "In the United States, we found compelling evidence that conservative states exhibited higher polarization around mask\nmandates and vaccines, with partisan division strongly negatively correlated with vaccination rates.", "page": 25},
          {"text": "The temporal patterns\nin our polarization metric successfully captured real-world political events", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The authors interpret the weaker Canadian relationships as evidence that a shared online information environment does not produce identical political or health outcomes: local institutions and political conditions may matter.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "This suggests that the impact of online polarization on public health behaviors may be mediated by broader political and institutional factors.", "page": 25},
          {"text": "even with exposure to the same\nsocial media platforms and significant content sharing across borders (with Canadians retweeting American content eight\ntimes more than Canadian content), the local political environment remains crucial in determining how online polarization\ntranslates to public opinion and behavior.", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For public-health communicators, monitoring which issue is dividing partisans in a given place and moment could be more useful than treating the public as a single national audience.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "For policymakers and public health officials, our work underscores the importance of understanding regional and partisan\ndifferences when designing communication strategies during health emergencies.", "page": 26},
          {"text": "The ability to identify particularly polarizing topics and track how polarization evolves in response to specific events could help authorities craft more effective\nmessaging that resonates across partisan divides.", "page": 26}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors sampled Twitter’s real-time stream from October 9, 2020, to January 4, 2021. Filtering left about 12.6 million U.S. tweets and 46.6 million Canadian tweets sharing COVID-19-related content.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We used Twitter’s (X) official API to collect 1% of real-time tweets for the United States and\nCanada from October 9, 2020 to January 4, 2021.", "page": 4},
          {"text": "This process yielded 12,552,213 tweets from 2,657,355 users for the United States and 46,636,206 tweets from\n1,757,675 users for Canada that shared COVID-19-related content.", "page": 5}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The three interventions were chosen to compare collective restrictions, individual precautions, and a medical intervention. Conspiracy discussion was coded separately and included refutation as well as endorsement; topic labels could overlap.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Our analysis focused on three specific pandemic-related topics: lockdowns, masks, and vaccines. These topics were\nselected for their salience in polarized discourse [27,43,44], and because they represent different types of interventions\n(group behavior, individual behavior, and medicine, respectively).", "page": 4},
          {"text": "For\nconspiracy-related tweets, relevant indicates that the content pertains to COVID-19 conspiracy theories, including tweets\nthat either promote the conspiracy or debunk / refute it, i.e., any tweet discussing the theory in either context. A tweet\ncould belong to multiple topics.", "page": 5}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "To identify relevant tweets beyond a fixed keyword list, political scientists labeled hashtags and reviewed sampled tweets. The authors then trained country-specific RoBERTa topic classifiers, weighting hashtag-derived labels less than manually verified ones.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Two political scientists manually annotated this list with topic and relevance labels, narrowing it to 631 relevant\nhashtags.", "page": 5},
          {"text": "Two political scientists independently reviewed each tweet to determine relevance, discarding\ntweets where consensus couldn’t be reached. We then fine-tuned the respective domain-adapted RoBERTa models to\nclassify by topics.", "page": 5},
          {"text": "During training, we also incorporated hashtag-annotated tweets as weak supervision, assigning them a reduced loss weight of 0.9 compared to 1.0 for\ngold-standard labels.", "page": 5}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Users were located from profile text when two mapping services agreed. Regional counts of geolocated users correlated with census populations at 0.98 in the U.S. and 0.92 in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We processed this free-form text field using Open Street Map and the ArcGIS API, which both return latitude and\nlongitude coordinates when a location is found. We established a clear geolocation when both APIs returned coordinates\nwithin one degree of each other", "page": 6},
          {"text": "we successfully geolocated 757,601 U.S. users with a strong correlation of 0.98 (n = 52, p = 9.27e-35, CI=[0.96,\n0.99]) to official population census data, and 282,454 Canadian users with a strong correlation of 0.92", "page": 6}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Explicit partisanship in profiles supplied training labels for a second classifier that inferred affiliation from users’ tweets. The U.S. comparison was Democratic versus Republican; the Canadian comparison combined five parties into two families.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We first identified politically explicit users based on their profile descriptions, using partisan keywords for each country’s major parties.", "page": 6},
          {"text": "For the activity classifier, we generated user embeddings by aggregating tweet embeddings\nfrom each user (using mean pooling), then trained a multi-layer perceptron to predict party affiliation.", "page": 8},
          {"text": "For the United States, we identified supporters of the Democratic (left) and Republican (right) parties. For Canada, we\ngrouped users into left (Liberal Party, New Democratic Party, and Green Party) and right (Conservative Party and People’s\nParty) partisan families.", "page": 4}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Affiliation estimates were checked against elections and individual voter records. Regional party ratios correlated about 0.80 with election results in both countries; agreement with matched U.S. voter records was lower, around 73–74%.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "we calculated the correlation between the ratio of liberal to\nconservative-labeled users per region in our dataset compared to official election results, obtaining strong correlations of\n0.802 for the United States and 0.815 for Canada", "page": 8},
          {"text": "we matched Twitter users with primary voter registration records available for five states (Ohio, New York,\nFlorida, Arkansas, North Carolina) and Washington DC.", "page": 8},
          {"text": "Our profile classifier achieved 74.35% accuracy and our activity classifier achieved 73.35% accuracy when compared\nto these official records.", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The unit of measurement was a user’s language on a topic, not a single tweet. The authors averaged that user’s tweet vectors and used the C-index to score how distinctly the two partisan groups clustered, on a scale from zero to one.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "To obtain a user embedding for a given topic, we aggregate all\nof the user’s relevant tweet embeddings by averaging.", "page": 10},
          {"text": "We then measure polarization by quantifying how far apart the language of left and right-leaning users is positioned\nin this embedding space, using the C-index [42], a robust clustering measure.", "page": 4},
          {"text": "This index ranges from 0 (no polarization) to 1 (extreme polarization where partisan groups are completely isolated from\neach other in the embedding space).", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For large groups, repeated subsampling made the distance calculation feasible. On daily lockdown and vaccine data, the approximation error fell below 0.001 at about 10,000 users.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Our algorithm (Algorithm 1) repeatedly samples a fraction of users, calculates the polarization index on this sample,\nand increases the sample size until the coefficient of variation falls below a specified threshold", "page": 11},
          {"text": "We validated our approximation method on daily lockdown and vaccine tweets. As shown in Fig 4, the absolute error\ndrops dramatically at around 3,000 users and falls below 0.001 at approximately 10,000 users.", "page": 11}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "For the geographic comparison, the authors averaged 12 weekly measurements, ranked regions by topic and overall, and compared the rankings with vote margins from the 2020 U.S. presidential and 2019 Canadian federal elections.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Color intensity from light to dark gives the amount of polarization measured weekly between October 11, 2020 to January 3, 2021 and then averaged\nover the 12 weeks.", "page": 13},
          {"text": "When examined against partisan voting patterns (using the 2020 U.S. presidential election and 2019 Canadian federal\nelection)", "page": 13},
          {"text": "with rankings applied separately to each topic (lockdowns, masks, vaccines) and aggregated as\nan “overall” score.", "page": 13}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The temporal analysis used daily national scores. It compared the series with events selected in advance, inspected the content at the two largest peaks per topic, and averaged across topics around identified events.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Our analysis of daily partisan polarization at the national level (Fig 13) reveals rapid daily fluctuations.", "page": 16},
          {"text": "pre-selected political and\nvaccine-related events (Table 8, shown as vertical lines in Fig 13)", "page": 16},
          {"text": "For each topic, we analyzed the two highest peaks, and inferred the content discussed on those peaks.", "page": 20},
          {"text": "we computed\naverage polarization scores across topics (Fig 14A,B) and performed event-triggered averaging.", "page": 19}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "External health measures included official case and death counts and the share receiving at least two vaccine doses. Uptake was measured in the corresponding period a year later, after vaccines became available.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We used officially reported COVID-19 new cases and death data from each country.", "page": 5},
          {"text": "The vaccination rates represent those who obtained at least two doses. For the United States, we use the\npeople_fully_vaccinated_per_hundred reported in the COVID Data Tracker from the CDC. For Canada, this is the\nnumtotal_fully from the government’s vaccine coverage map.", "page": 5},
          {"text": "The\nVaccination Rate is also averaged weekly for the similar period of time a year into future to be after the vaccines\nwere rolled out, i.e., October 11, 2021 to January 3, 2022.", "page": 26}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "More conservative regions generally showed larger partisan divides, but the association depended on the topic and country. Republican vote share was significantly associated with polarization over masks and vaccines, not lockdowns; the Canadian vote-margin associations were not significant. Delaware, Idaho, Alabama, and Arkansas illustrate why voting patterns alone do not explain the rankings.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "We find\nstrong, significant correlations in the US between Republican vote share and polarization around masks and vaccines,\nthough not for lockdowns. For Canada, these associations are not statistically significant, partly due to the smaller number\nof regions analyzed.", "page": 16},
          {"text": "The lockdown discourse shows a more mixed pattern, with outliers from both liberal\nand conservative states; namely Idaho, Alabama, and Arkansas showing the least polarization, and Delaware (ranked 1),\nColorado (ranked 17), and New Jersey (ranked 15) showing higher values.", "page": 14},
          {"text": "Canada, while showing similar patterns, demonstrated significantly lower overall polarization levels.", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "U.S. states more divided over vaccines in 2020 had lower subsequent vaccination rates (r = −0.77). Canada did not reproduce that inverse relationship: excluding Nunavut yielded a positive correlation, which the authors caution rests on only 12 regions.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "we confirm a strong negative correlation (r = −0.77, p < 0.001) between\nvaccine polarization and vaccination rates.", "page": 16},
          {"text": "We remove Nunavut as an outlier because of its very small population. The correlation is 0.74 with CI = [0.31, 0.92] (n = 12, p = 0.004).", "page": 26},
          {"text": "However, with the few points, we do not have\na definite conclusion for this result.", "page": 26}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Polarization often rose and fell within days around identifiable events. The U.S. vaccine debate peaked just after Biden’s vaccination, while Canada’s largest lockdown peak coincided with a Toronto protest against health measures.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "This aggregate analysis (Fig\n14c) confirms a rapid, symmetric rise-and-decay profile around polarization peaks, occurring on timescales of days.", "page": 19},
          {"text": "The second-largest\npeak occurred on December 22, 2020, the day after Biden received his first COVID-19 vaccine [54].", "page": 18},
          {"text": "In Canada’s lockdown discourse (Fig 13B), the highest peak occurred on October 17, 2020, coinciding with a large\nToronto anti-mask protest against COVID-19 measures.", "page": 18}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Conservatives discussed conspiracy theories more frequently in both countries, but more conspiracy talk did not mean greater daily partisan separation. The association was weakly negative in the U.S. and statistically indistinguishable from zero in Canada.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "show that conservative partisans in both countries tweet conspiracy-related content at higher rates than progressive\npartisans.", "page": 21},
          {"text": "When correlated with aggregate daily polarization (Fig 15 B and D, we find a small but significant negative correlation\nfor the United States, while no significant correlation was observed for Canada.", "page": 21}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Daily aggregate polarization did not track pandemic severity in either country when compared with reported cases and deaths.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Here, we investigate the aggregated polarization over time for each country and how it relates to the reported number of New Cases and Deaths for\nCOVID-19.", "page": 27},
          {"text": "observe that polarization is not correlated with the severity of the pandemic, in both the United States and Canada.", "page": 27}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "Semantic distance is a proxy, not a direct observation of disagreement. Averaging by user can obscure sarcasm, indirect disagreement, and other meanings not captured by the embeddings.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "we measured partisan polarization only through the proxy of semantic similarity, which may in certain cases obscure signals not captured by semantic\nembedding representations.", "page": 24},
          {"text": "Because our measure aggregates tweets into user-level embeddings, it captures\noverall partisan alignment at the user level, rather than fine-grained nuances in individual tweets.", "page": 10},
          {"text": "subtle rhetorical strategies such as sarcasm or indirect disagreement may be less apparent", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The Canadian activity classifier could not reliably distinguish parties on the same side. The analysis therefore does not resolve within-family differences or represent the Bloc Québécois and other excluded parties.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "the F1-score was not\nsatisfactory as parties within the liberal (left) party family and conservative (right) party family was easily confused as\nshown in the confusion matrix in Table 5. Hence, we grouped the parties based on their partisan leaning.", "page": 8},
          {"text": "We also\nremoved supporters of other minor parties and the Bloc Quebecois.", "page": 8}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Party labels depend partly on what users report about themselves. Voter records are an imperfect check because registrations can be stale and names do not always match.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Additionally, our classification into partisan groups relies\non self-reported information, which may not be entirely accurate.", "page": 24},
          {"text": "voter registrations may be outdated or not fully reflect\ncurrent partisan leanings expressed online.", "page": 10},
          {"text": "name-based matching can miss users due to nicknames, misspellings, or common names", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The findings describe a politically engaged subset of Twitter users, not the distribution of opinions in the U.S. or Canadian public.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Twitter users are more politically engaged and often more partisan than the general population, which means our results reflect dynamics within a vocal subset of online political discourse.", "page": 24}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "English-only sampling leaves out much francophone discussion and makes Quebec’s high measured polarization especially difficult to interpret.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "we restricted\nour analysis to English-language tweets, which in Canada means we primarily captured perspectives of either anglophones or bilingual francophones — a potential source of bias.", "page": 24},
          {"text": "For example, the high polarization observed in Quebec\nregarding COVID-19 measures may be influenced by this language limitation.", "page": 24}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Location is inferred rather than verified. Inaccurate or fabricated profiles, VPNs, and automated accounts can undermine geographic attribution; bots may also enlarge the apparent partisan divide.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "self-reported Twitter locations may be incomplete, imprecise, or even intentionally false\n(including the rare influence of VPNs); and that automated accounts (bots) can provide misleading location details and\ngenerate highly partisan content, which may artificially inflate observed partisan divides.", "page": 10}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The national corpora were collected differently: U.S. keywords emphasized the election, while Canadian keywords more directly targeted COVID-19 and provinces. Some apparent country differences may reflect that sampling choice.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "The U.S. keyword\nlist was dominated by election-related terms, then filtered for COVID-19 terms. By contrast, the Canadian keyword list\nincorporated province names and explicit COVID-related identifiers, which likely offered stronger pandemic relevance.", "page": 25},
          {"text": "This asymmetry may have contributed to cross-national differences in patterns of observed polarization", "page": 25}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Canada’s small number of regions limits inference about both vote margins and vaccine uptake. The authors also identify Canadian vaccine mandates as a reason not to expect a straightforward comparison with the U.S.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "For Canada, these associations are not statistically significant, partly due to the smaller number\nof regions analyzed.", "page": 16},
          {"text": "We did not observe a similar pattern in Canada, due to small sample size and the implementation of vaccine mandates.", "page": 16}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Late-2020 tweets and later regional vaccination rates are not contemporaneous observations. Their correlation may have predictive value, but it does not establish that online division reduced uptake or isolate its effect from ideology, education, or other regional factors.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "As a result, correlations between polarization in vaccine-related discussions\nand later vaccination uptake should be understood as suggestive rather than causative.", "page": 25},
          {"text": "While the exact causal mechanism remains\nunclear, factors such as education and political ideology, which also have strong geographic dependencies, likely played\nsignificant roles [ 60].", "page": 23}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Matching peaks to news or policy announcements establishes timing, not causation. An external event could not be identified for every peak; online posts themselves are a possible explanation.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "While direct causal evidence is not available, we find that many of the largest polarization peaks coincide with\nhighly contentious events specific to each country’s context.", "page": 17},
          {"text": "While no external event was\nidentified on October 31, 2020 (highest peak), we found highly polarized social media discourse", "page": 17},
          {"text": "This is possibly an\nexample of influencer post-driven, rather than real world event-driven polarization.", "page": 18}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study does not settle why conservative regions were often more polarized or quantify how much cross-border circulation and social networks themselves changed attitudes or behavior.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "our analysis did not identify a single explanation for the relationship between conservative politics and higher\npolarization", "page": 23},
          {"text": "the role of cross-border\ninformation flows in shaping polarization deserves further investigation", "page": 26},
          {"text": "quantifying\ntheir precise impact on polarization and subsequent public health outcomes remains an important challenge for future\nwork.", "page": 26}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The authors extend research on partisan pandemic responses: rather than identifying a general left–right difference, they examine how its magnitude varies geographically and changes around events.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "In line with previous research ((e.g., [17,58–60])), we found that more right leaning states and provinces exhibited\ngreater partisan divisions around COVID-19 on Twitter (X), particularly concerning mask mandates and vaccine\ndistribution.", "page": 22},
          {"text": "However, we extended beyond these studies to characterize the geographic heterogeneity and temporal\nevolution of polarization, connecting features in our polarization metric to real-world events.", "page": 22}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Within computational research on polarization, the authors adopt a text-based alternative to follower and retweet networks. They do not reject network measures as ineffective; they choose text because access to network data is increasingly restricted.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "network-based approaches such as inferring ideology\nfrom follower or retweet structures have proven highly effective in prior work [37,38,39].", "page": 3},
          {"text": "However, access to user- and\nnetwork-level data has become increasingly restricted, particularly under recent platform policy changes. Consequently,\nour study adopts a strictly text-based method, which remains both accessible and scalable for analyzing large-scale digital\ndiscourse.", "page": 3}
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "They qualify the linguistic account of polarization by comparing partisans within a shared contentious topic. They combine contextual language representations with a clustering measure, treating separation in meaning as an indicator rather than equating any vocabulary difference with disagreement.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "when examining discussions on the same contentious topic, language dissimilarity between different partisan\ngroups more likely reflects alternative semantic understandings of that specific topic—a phenomenon we hypothesize\ncorrelates strongly with polarization.", "page": 4},
          {"text": "Specifically, we transform social media posts using RoBERTa, an open-source transformer-based language model\n[41]. We then measure polarization by quantifying how far apart the language of left and right-leaning users is positioned\nin this embedding space, using the C-index [42], a robust clustering measure.", "page": 4}
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Eric Merkley and colleagues reported unusual agreement across Canadian party lines on early emergency measures. Yang and colleagues qualify the reach of that finding: agreement in the broader political response did not mean that Canadian social-media discussion was similarly unified.",
        "quotes": [
          {"text": "Indeed, there was a rare “crosspartisan consensus” among Canadians regarding emergency measures in the early stages of the pandemic [103]. This\nconsensus, however, was not mirrored on social media, where conspiracy theories widely circulated [24,95].", "page": 24},
          {"text": "Merkley E, Bridgman A, Loewen PJ, Owen T, Ruths D, Zhilin O. A rare moment of cross-partisan consensus: Elite and public response to the\nCOVID-19 pandemic in Canada.", "page": 31}
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
